<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cetinje Octoechos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1494">1494</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Oktoih prvoglasnik</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetinje">Cetinje</a> Octoechos, or Oktoih, is a book of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> liturgical hymns printed in 1494 by the monk Makarije at the press of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durad-crnojevic">Đurađ Crnojević</a> in Cetinje. It is the first book printed in Cyrillic among the South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> and one of the earliest Cyrillic printed books anywhere. Only a few copies survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cetinje-octoechos.jpg" alt="Cetinje Octoechos" width="308" height="561" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oktoih01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Cetinje Octoechos, in Serbian Cetinjski oktoih, was printed in Cetinje, then the capital of the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeta-region">Zeta</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a>. It was made under the direction of Hieromonk Makarije at the Crnojević printing house, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durad-crnojevic">Đurađ Crnojević</a>, the ruler of Zeta, had founded in 1493, so that the first volume was finished within about a year of the press's founding. It is the first incunabulum in the Serbian recension of Church Slavonic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The book was printed in two instalments, following the eight tones of the Octoechos system of musical modes. The first volume, the Octoechos of the First Tone (Oktoih prvoglasnik), holds the hymns sung to the first four tones, and the second, the Octoechos of the Fifth Tone (Oktoih petoglasnik), those for the remaining four.</p><p>The first volume was finished on 4 January 1494. It has 270 leaves measuring 29 by 21.6 centimetres, printed cleanly in red and black with finely formed letters, and is decorated with headpieces and initials cut in wood in the spirit of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, with traces of the older <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> tradition. In the quality of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> and ornament it is judged equal to the Venetian work of its time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Octoechos of the Fifth Tone is the first illustrated printed book in Serbian Cyrillic and the second South Slavic incunabulum. It survives only in fragments, the longest of 37 leaves, and has six woodcut illustrations whose artist fitted complex scenes with many figures into a small space. In 1987 the National Library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a>, Đurđe Crnojević, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetinje">Cetinje</a> published 600 facsimiles of the Octoechos of the First Tone.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cetinje Octoechos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son Đurađ opened a printing press that produced the <b>Oktoih</b> in 1494, among the first Cyrillic books printed.</blockquote><a href="/state/zeta-under-the-crnojevici">Zeta under the Crnojevići</a></li></ul></section>
